      "narrative": null,
      "content": "12\nWEEKLY EPIDEMIOLOGICAL RECORD, NO. 2, 14 JANUARY 2000\nThiomersal as a vaccine preservative\nWHO supports a statement made on 7 July 1999 by the\nAmerican Academy of Pediatrics1 and the United States\nPublic Health Service regarding prospective phasing out of\nthiomersal û a chemical compound containing mercury\nused in trace amounts in certain vaccine manufacturing\nprocesses and as a vaccine preservative. However, the\nOrganization underlines the importance of continuing to\nuse currently available childrenÆs vaccines containing\nthiomersal. Thiomersal (also known as thimerosal or\nmercurothiolate)",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A bilingual Weekly Epidemiological Record item on thiomersal as a vaccine preservative, in which WHO supports the 1999 US statement on phasing it out while urging continued use of current children's vaccines that contain it. It explains thiomersal's long use against contamination of multidose vials and that no serious adverse events have been attributed to it. It reviews what is known about ethylmercury and methylmercury toxicity and promotes a preservative-free hepatitis B device for newborns. The French portion, with garbled accented characters, discusses the costs and supply problems of removing thiomersal, especially for developing countries.",
